# FREAD(3S)

## NAME

fread, fwrite - buffered binary input/output

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

size_t fread(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nitems, FILE *stream);

size_t fwrite(const void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nitems, FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Fread* reads, into a block beginning at *ptr, nitems* of data of *size* bytes each from the
named input *stream*. It returns the number of items actually read.

*Fwrite* appends at most *nitems* of data of *size* bytes each beginning at *ptr* to the named
output *stream*. It returns the number of items actually written.

## SEE ALSO

read(2), write(2), fopen(3), getc(3), putc(3), gets(3), puts(3), printf(3), scanf(3), stdio(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Fread* and *fwrite* return 0 upon end of file or error.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**Size is a byte count, not a word count. sizeof(int)** is **6**, six char-units, because six
characters pack into one 48-bit word - so **fread(buf, sizeof(int), n, f)** moves 6n bytes and
not n of anything else. This is the same arithmetic that makes getw(3) move six bytes rather
than the PDP-11's two.

**size_t is signed here.** That is a deliberate departure from C11 sec 7.19, recorded in
include/README.md, and it is why a mixed comparison against a difference of two counts behaves.

